# The Star Weaver's Gift

In the time before time, when darkness blanketed the world, there lived a lonely weaver named Aria who dwelt at the edge of the void. Her fingers were skilled, but she had nothing to weave except shadows and silence.

One night, she wept tears of such pure sorrow that they crystallized as they fell, becoming brilliant points of light. Amazed, Aria gathered these luminous drops and began to weave them into the fabric of the sky.

She worked for countless ages, crying tears of joy, wonder, sadness, and hope. Each emotion created different colored lights—white for joy, red for passion, blue for melancholy. She wove them into patterns: great bears, hunters, swans, and dragons dancing across the heavens.

But Aria grew weary and knew her time was ending. With her final breath, she wove herself into her greatest creation—a river of light flowing across the sky, binding all her stars together.

Now, when people gaze upward at the Milky Way, they see Aria's final masterpiece. And on the clearest nights, if you look carefully at the stars, you might glimpse the tears of the Star Weaver still shimmering, reminding us that even from sorrow, beauty can be born.
